 Flood (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Nebraska's 1st District leans Republican by about six points, and Mike Flood is seeking a third full term with the advantages of incumbency and a large financial edge, having raised roughly $2.4 million to Chris Backemeyer's $590,000. Every professional rating on the race favors Flood, with most calling it Likely Republican and several going further to Solid or Safe.\n\nStill, the contest shows signs of life. A Democratic-sponsored September poll of likely voters had the candidates essentially tied at 43-42, a sharp contrast with spring surveys that put Flood ahead by seven to eight points. The two have met in debates covering Iran, tariffs, and rising prices, with each side claiming the edge, and Backemeyer argues for change while Flood touts his record. Prediction markets still price Flood's chances around 85 percent. The forecast expects Flood to win by a mid-single-digit margin, with uncertainty elevated because recent independent polling is thin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1466, "margin": -6.26}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1237, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1206, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2177, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1689, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.1196, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1777, "margin": -6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2315, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.1394, "margin": -7.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.1156, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.114, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.1142, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.1121, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.173, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-06", "p_dem": 0.1374, "margin": -6.6}]}